1
00:00:02,039 --> 00:00:09,879
I merely repeat, remember always your duty of enmity towards man and all his ways.

2
00:00:09,879 --> 00:00:13,600
Whatever goes upon two legs is an enemy.

3
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:18,159
Whatever goes upon four legs or has wings is a friend.

4
00:00:18,159 --> 00:00:24,679
And remember also that in fighting against man, we must not come to resemble him.

5
00:00:24,679 --> 00:00:29,120
Even when you have conquered him, do not adopt his vices.

6
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:34,759
No animal must ever live in a house or sleep in a bed or wear clothes or drink

7
00:00:34,759 --> 00:00:40,360
alcohol or smoke tobacco or touch money or engage in trade.

8
00:00:40,360 --> 00:00:44,000
All the habits of man are evil.

9
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,799
And above all, no animal must ever tyrannize over his own kind.

10
00:00:49,799 --> 00:00:55,120
Weak or strong, clever or simple, we are all brothers.

11
00:00:55,120 --> 00:00:58,879
No animal must ever kill any other animal.

12
00:00:58,879 --> 00:01:01,399
All animals are equal.

13
00:01:01,399 --> 00:01:06,480
And now, comrades, I will tell you about my dream of last night.

14
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:09,360
I cannot describe that dream to you.

15
00:01:09,360 --> 00:01:14,200
It was a dream of the earth as it will be when man has vanished.

16
00:01:14,200 --> 00:01:17,125
But it reminded me of something that I had long forgotten.

17
00:01:17,125 --> 00:01:20,080
>> [clears throat] >> Many years ago, when I was a little pig,

18
00:01:20,080 --> 00:01:24,440
my mother and the other sows used to sing an old song of which they knew only

19
00:01:24,440 --> 00:01:27,799
the tune and the first three words.

20
00:01:27,799 --> 00:01:33,839
I had known that tune in my infancy, but it had long since passed out of my mind.

21
00:01:33,839 --> 00:01:39,080
Last night, however, it came back to me in my dream. And what is more, the words

22
00:01:39,080 --> 00:01:44,559
of the song also came back. Words, I am certain, which were sung by the animals

23
00:01:44,559 --> 00:01:49,399
of long ago and have been lost to memory for generations.

24
00:01:49,399 --> 00:01:52,559
I will sing you that song now, comrades.

25
00:01:52,559 --> 00:01:55,600
I am old and my voice is hoarse.

26
00:01:55,600 --> 00:02:00,440
But when I have taught you the tune, you can sing it better for yourselves.

27
00:02:00,440 --> 00:02:03,919
It is called Beasts of England.

28
00:02:03,919 --> 00:02:07,799
>> Old Major cleared his throat and began to sing.

29
00:02:07,799 --> 00:02:12,679
As he had said, his voice was hoarse, but he sang well enough, and it was a

30
00:02:12,679 --> 00:02:14,679
stirring tune.

31
00:02:14,679 --> 00:02:19,679
Something between Clementine and La Cucaracha.

32
00:02:19,679 --> 00:02:26,519
The words ran >> [clears throat] >> Beasts of England, beasts of Ireland,

33
00:02:26,519 --> 00:02:32,919
beasts of every land and clime, hearken to my joyful tidings of the golden

34
00:02:32,919 --> 00:02:39,679
future time. Soon or late, the day is coming, tyrant man shall be overthrown,

35
00:02:39,679 --> 00:02:44,840
and the fruitful fields of England shall be trod by beasts alone.

36
00:02:44,840 --> 00:02:49,919
Rings shall vanish from our noses and the harness from our back.

37
00:02:49,919 --> 00:02:57,200
Bit and spur shall rust forever. Cruel whips no more shall crack. Riches more

38
00:02:57,200 --> 00:03:05,159
than mind can picture, wheat and barley, oats and hay, clover, beans, and mangel-wurzels

39
00:03:05,159 --> 00:03:07,679
shall be ours upon that day.

40
00:03:07,679 --> 00:03:12,639
Bright will shine the fields of England, purer shall its waters be, >> [music]

41
00:03:12,639 --> 00:03:17,600
>> sweeter yet shall blow its breezes on the day that sets us free.

42
00:03:17,600 --> 00:03:22,440
For that day we all must labor, though we die before it break.

43
00:03:22,440 --> 00:03:27,519
Cows and horses, [music] geese and turkeys, all must toil for freedom's

44
00:03:27,519 --> 00:03:34,559
sake. Beasts of England, beasts of Ireland, beasts of every land and clime,

45
00:03:34,559 --> 00:03:40,360
hearken well and spread my tidings of the golden future time.

46
00:03:40,360 --> 00:03:45,440
>> The singing of this song threw the animals into the wildest excitement.

47
00:03:45,440 --> 00:03:50,640
Almost before Major had reached the end, they had begun singing it for themselves.

48
00:03:50,640 --> 00:03:56,119
Even the stupidest of them had already picked up the tune and a few of the words.

49
00:03:56,119 --> 00:04:02,480
And as for the clever ones, such as the pigs and dogs, they had the entire song

50
00:04:02,480 --> 00:04:05,519
by heart within a few minutes.

51
00:04:05,519 --> 00:04:12,359
And then, after a few preliminary tries, the whole farm burst out into Beasts of

52
00:04:12,359 --> 00:04:16,079
England in tremendous unison.

53
00:04:16,079 --> 00:04:22,680
The cows lowed it, the dogs whined it, the sheep bleated it, the horses

54
00:04:22,680 --> 00:04:26,519
whinnied it, the ducks quacked it.

55
00:04:26,519 --> 00:04:32,680
They were so delighted with the song that they sang it right through five times in succession.

56
00:04:32,680 --> 00:04:38,000
And might have continued singing it all night if they had not been interrupted.
